<NeoMyco title="Know Your Woods — NEO Myco" description="Keep a fungal inventory of your own yard or a nearby trail: one place, visited again and again. Why it matters, and how NEO Myco members will build on it.">
<section class="wrap section guide">
  <p class="kicker">NEO MYCO / KNOW YOUR WOODS</p>
  <h1>A fungal inventory of a place <em>you already know.</em></h1>
  <p class="lead">Your yard. The trail you walk every week. Know Your Woods is a simple idea: pick one place, keep returning, and record every fungus you find there. Over time, that place starts to tell you things.</p>
  <p>It’s the everyday version of what NEO Myco is about, and you can start now, before the club launches in 2027.</p>
  <div class="hero-actions"><a class="button" href="/one-place/#get-started">Start your inventory: step-by-step guide <span>→</span></a><a class="text-link" href="#why">Why it’s worth doing ↓</a></div>
</section>

<section class="wrap section" id="what">
  <div class="section-heading"><p class="kicker">01 / WHAT IT IS</p><h2>One place. <em>Every fungus. Over time.</em></h2></div>
  <div class="intro"><p class="lead">A fungal inventory is a running list of the fungi recorded in one defined place, built from repeat visits.</p><p>What makes it useful isn’t the length of the list. It’s that you search the same area each time and note how you looked: the date, the weather, your route and how long you spent. That turns “I saw this once” into a record you can compare from season to season and year to year. A visit with no mushrooms counts too.</p></div>
  <div class="practice-grid kyw-kinds">
    <article><span class="number">AT HOME</span><h3>Your yard or property</h3><p>The easiest place to return to, at any hour and in any weather. Lawns, mulch beds, tree roots and woodpiles all host different fungi. You decide what you can collect.</p></article>
    <article><span class="number">NEARBY</span><h3>A trail you can walk often</h3><p>Choose a fixed route or a defined loop in a park or preserve and stick to it. Check the site’s rules: permission to walk and photograph is not permission to collect.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="mission" id="why"><div class="wrap section">
  <p class="kicker">02 / WHY IT’S WORTH DOING</p>
  <div class="section-heading"><h2>Small place. <em>Real science.</em></h2></div>
  <ol class="pipeline">{reasons.map(([n,title,body])=><li><span class="number">{n}</span><h3>{title}</h3><p>{body}</p></li>)}</ol>
</div></section>

<section class="wrap section" id="club">
  <div class="section-heading"><p class="kicker">03 / HOW IT CONNECTS TO NEO MYCO</p><h2>Your inventory, <em>part of a bigger picture.</em></h2></div>
  <div class="intro"><p class="lead">At club events, our mission is to sequence everything we find, except <NeoTerm term="oversampled taxa" id="oversampled-kyw">Species that already have plenty of reliable DNA sequences in public databases, usually common and easy-to-recognize fungi. One more sequence of them adds little, so we spend sequencing on finds that can still teach us something.</NeoTerm>. Home and trail inventories are where many of the most interesting finds will first turn up.</p><p>Bring standout finds from your inventory to club events, compare notes with other members, and learn how to voucher a specimen so it can be sequenced. Each member’s place adds to a shared picture of Northeast Ohio’s fungi.</p></div>
  <div class="return-record"><Photo src="inocybe-communis-front-yard-2026.jpg" alt="Inocybe communis from MycoGeeky’s front yard" loading="lazy" display={760} /><div><p class="kicker">A PLACE TO BEGIN</p><h2>It can start at home.</h2><p>MycoGeeky’s front-yard inventory connects a familiar place to a newly described species. See how the records, the DNA barcode and the publication fit together.</p><a href="/neo-myco/#field-record">Open the <i>Inocybe communis</i> field record →</a></div></div>
</section>

<section class="join wrap">
  <p class="kicker">READY WHEN YOU ARE</p>
  <h2>Pick your place. <br/><em>Make the first visit.</em></h2>
